# Creational Patterns: Singleton & Factory

Creational design patterns deal with object creation mechanisms to create objects in a manner suitable to the situation.

---

## 1. Singleton Pattern
Ensures a class has **only one instance** and provides a global point of access to it (e.g., Database Connection Pool, Logger, Configuration Manager).

### Thread-Safe Implementation (Double-Checked Locking in Java):
```java
public class DatabaseConnection {
    private static volatile DatabaseConnection instance;

    private DatabaseConnection() {} // Private constructor

    public static DatabaseConnection getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (DatabaseConnection.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new DatabaseConnection();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Factory Method Pattern
Defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate.
- Decouples client code from concrete product classes.
